@meta
id: 10-20
title: Drill — pick the right delimiter
chapter: 10
type: drill
skills: obj-paren, obj-brace, obj-quote
par: 58
minutes: 6
reps: 6
vscode: three selections, three chances to catch a bracket
open: notify.ts

@brief
Six times: every one of these three lines now takes the whole `payload`
instead of what it currently holds. Same edit, three different
delimiters — round, curly, square.

Nothing to work out. The drill is the half-second where you look at the
line and your hand already knows which bracket it is.

@keys
| `ci(` | inside the round brackets |
| `ci{` | inside the curly braces — keep the padding spaces |
| `ci[` | inside the square brackets |

@hints
Get onto the bracket first with `f`, then the object.
--
`f( ci(`, `f{ ci{`, `f[ ci[`. The curly one wants a space either side of
`payload` to match the file's style.

@solution
    j f( ci( payload <Esc>
    j 0 f{ ci{  payload  <Esc>
    j 0 f[ ci[ payload <Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Fifty keystrokes for three edits, and the only thing that changed between
them was one character of the command.

That is the payoff for learning the family rather than the members. There
is no separate technique for arrays; there is one technique and four
delimiters. By the end of the reps you should not be deciding which object
to use, only reading which bracket is on the screen.

@start notify.ts
export function enqueue(payload: NotifyPayload) {
  queue.push(customer.email, 'order-shipped');
  track({ locale: 'en-GB', currency: 'GBP' });
  return retryable['REFUND_FAILED'];
}

@expected notify.ts
export function enqueue(payload: NotifyPayload) {
  queue.push(payload);
  track({ payload });
  return retryable[payload];
}
